    "q-162761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162761",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-1)=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=1 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1+1)=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the point $(1,0)$ and its minimum value is $0.$",
